2026-05-28 02:12:20,759 - openai._base_client - DEBUG - Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/chat/completions', 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-80af2d26-bb03-4519-b30c-6e7459f40319', 'security': {'bearer_auth': True}, 'content': None, 'json_data': {'messages': [{'role': 'user', 'content': '\n    Context:  rate of building damage\noccurred decreased by 17%.\n4 Online Tourism Resource Development\nPractices\n4.1 Comparative Case Analysis\nThrough the comparison of the effects of digitalization and tourism\ndevelopment of typical cases such as Huangshan Anti-Japanese War\nsite group, Liziba Park and Shanchengxiang (Table 5), the following\nthree common rules can be found.\n4.1.1 Technology-Driven Restoration Efficiency. Improvement\nHigh-def 3D scanning can improve restoration efficiency by 33%,\nbut the model precision needed to be balanced with real-time\nrendering performance (such as millions of polygons for a\nbuilding).\n4.1.2 Double-Edged Cultural Expression. Dynamic scene replica-\ntion can improve the understanding of history by more than 50%,\nbut excessive commercialization (such as punch point of Internet\ncelebrity) will reduce the number of EEG+ emotional signals by\n40%.\n556\x0cDigital Preservation of Chongqing Red Culture and Tourism Resource Development in Bayu Traditional Dwellings DSAI 2025, May 23–25, 2025, Wuhan, China\nTable 6: Intergenerational Comparison of Conservation Technologies.\nTechnological\nGeneration\nRepresentative Methods Data Dimensions Error Rate Storage\nDuration\nInformation\nInteractivity\n1st (1930s) Paper-based Surveying 2D Planar 5-8% 50-80 years Unidirectional\nRecording\n2nd (1980s) Total Station + Film\nPhotography\n2.5D Facades (Elevation +\nPartial Depth Data)\n2-3% 30-50 years Limited Retrieval\n3rd (2020s) Laser Scanning + Digital Twin 5D Spacetime (3D + Time\n+ Environment)\n0.5% Permanent\nCloud Storage\nReal-time\nInteraction\n4.1.3 Living Heritage Transmission Crisis. The return rate of in-\ndigenous people is generally below 30%, and intangible cultural\nheritage skills are at risk of interruption in inheritance. It is neces-\nsary to achieve a closed loop of ”protection - benefit - participation”\nthrough policy guidance.\n4.2 Model bottlenecks and optimization\nrecommendations\n4.2.1 International cooperation experience. The ”government-led +\ncommunity crowdfunding” model used by Eastern European coun-\ntries such as Bulgaria and Hungary for historical buildings preser-\nvation can provide institutional design references for Chongqing.\nTsyganov (2022) found that Bulgaria’s digital authorization   standard of Digital Protection of Intangible Cultural\nHeritage (2023) [3].It reconstructs historical scene such as bomb-\ning scene with temporal-space information. Historical knowledge\nretention rate has been increased by 58%.\n1.2.3 Industrial Convergence. A digital twin platform for home-\nstay in rural tourism. Homestay price increased 126% (200 →480\nCNY/night), and online cultural revenue accounted for 41% of total\nincome (Ciqikou Ancient Town case).\n2 Current Status of Technologies for Data\nAcquisition and Modeling\n2.1 Data Acquisition and Modeling\nTechnologies\n2.1.1 3D Laser Scanning. FARO Focus S350 realizes millimeter-\nlevel point cloud modeling. The thermal map of structural damage\nis generated with an average error margin <0.5% (Table 1) and meets\nthe requirements of heritage monitoring by UNESCO[4].\n2.1.2 Drone Oblique Photography. Drone Oblique Photography\nImages acquired from the air with 50-150m height cover multi-angle\nviews (Figure 4). The textured 3D model is generated by Context\nCapture software based on the stitching of images. The decay con-\ndition of buildings can be identified, for example, the area of wood\ndarkening suggests corrosion. Based on GIS software, the spatial\nFigure 4: Aerial Photography of Peng’s Traditional Dwelling\nby UA V. Photograph by Yue Yang.\ndistribution of buildings can be analyzed. The model is not only the\nrepresentation of the appearance of buildings, but also the reflection\nof the material condition through the color of the buildings, for ex-\nample, the area of wood darkening represents the risk of corrosion\n(Figure 5)[5]. Finally, based on the electronic map technology (GIS),\nthe scattered 3D models are aggregated into spatial distribution\nmaps. The building density, the age distribution, and other impor-\ntant data are intuitively displayed by the map, which provides a\nscientific basis for the planning of heritage conservation[6].\n2.1.3 Dynamic data   integration. Digital Preservation of Intangible\nCultural Heritage: Digital Resource Collection and Cataloguing\n(WH/T 99.9-2023) Part 9: Traditional Craftsmanship, the craftsman-\nship process such as wooden construction and rammed earth tech-\nnology are recorded in sequence. Then, an interactive knowledge\ngraph is constructed to digitalize the recording of craftsmanship\nprocess.\n2.2 Digital Twin Platform Development\n2.2.1 Unity3D Engine Application. A digital twin system realizes\nthe real-time monitoring of visitors and AR virtual tour (Figure 6).\nThe historical cognition of visitors increased by 53% after the QR\ncode animation triggered by Unity3D engine.\n2.2.2 Metaverse Scenario Construction. Based on the NetEase Yao-\ntai architecture, a co-creation module of “Red Journey” is con-\nstructed in the virtual village. Users can upload wartime-themed\n552\x0cDigital Preservation of Chongqing Red Culture and Tourism Resource Development in Bayu Traditional Dwellings DSAI 2025, May 23–25, 2025, Wuhan, China\nTable 1: Core Data of Digital Preservation for Anti-Japanese War Vernacular Dwellings in Chongqing.\nBuilding Name Structure Type Termite\nIssue\nDigital Status Modeling Precision Data Error\nRate\nTechnical Methods\nHuangshan\nOfficial\nResidence\nBrick-Wood\nHybrid\nPresent Completed Millimeter-level\n(LOD400)\n0.32% FARO Laser Scanning +\nUA V Oblique Photography\nLiziba War Relics\nSite\nWooden\nStructure\nPresent Completed Millimeter-level\n(LOD350)\n0.45% Leica RTC360 + IoT\nHumidity Compensation\nZengjiayan\nNo.50\nBrick-Wood\nHybrid\nPresent Completed Submillimeter-level 0.28% Phase-Shift Scanning +\nHyperspectral Mapping\nBai Mansion Reinforced\nConcrete\nAbsent Completed Millimeter-level\n(LOD300)\n0.39% BIM Reverse Modeling +\nPoint Cloud Registration\nZhou Enlai’s\nFormer\nResidence\nWooden\nStructure\nPresent Completed Millimeter-level\n(LOD400)\n0.31% Multi-Station Point Cloud\nFusion + Structured Light\nNanshan War\nRelics\nMixed Structure Present Completed Centimeter-level\n(LOD150)\n0.78% Handheld Scanner +\nPanoramic Photography\nCiqikou Ancient\nTown\nWooden\nStructure\nPresent Completed Millimeter-level\n(LOD350)\n0.41% Mobile SLAM + Temporal\nModeling\nFotuguan War\nRelics\nStone-Wood\nHybrid\nAbsent Completed Millimeter-level\n(LOD300)\n0.37% UA V Swarm Scanning +\nEdge Computing\nLianglukou War\nRelics\nMixed Structure Present Completed Centimeter-level\n(LOD200)\n0.62% Lightweight Modeling +\nUGC Data Supplement\nFigure 5: 3D models of Peng’s Traditional Dwelling. Photo-\ngraph by Yue Yang.\nphotography and hand-painted drawings. The dynamic digital\narchive is generated by the uploaded files and connects the histori-\ncal narrative and  \n\n    Claim: 1st (1930s) generation method is Paper-based Surveying with 2D Planar data dimensions.\n\n    Task:\n    You are a strict data auditor. Evaluate the claim strictly against the provided context only. Do not use external knowledge.\n\n    Rate the claim using one of the following categories:\n    - Supported (output 5): The claim is directly stated or explicitly affirmed in the context.\n    - Strongly implied (output 4): The claim is not stated word‑for‑word, but can be logically and clearly inferred from multiple explicit statements in the context.\n    - Not mentioned (output 3): The context does not contain enough information to determine whether the claim