ate of in-\ndigenous people is generally below 30%, and intangible cultural\nheritage skills are at risk of interruption in inheritance. It is neces-\nsary to achieve a closed loop of ”protection - benefit - participation”\nthrough policy guidance.\n4.2 Model bottlenecks and optimization\nrecommendations\n4.2.1 International cooperation experience. The ”government-led +\ncommunity crowdfunding” model used by Eastern European coun-\ntries such as Bulgaria and Hungary for historical buildings preser-\nvation can provide institutional design references for Chongqing.\nTsyganov (2022) found that Bulgaria’s digital authorization   standard of Digital Protection of Intangible Cultural\nHeritage (2023) [3].It reconstructs historical scene such as bomb-\ning scene with temporal-space information. Historical knowledge\nretention rate has been increased by 58%.\n1.2.3 Industrial Convergence. A digital twin platform for home-\nstay in rural tourism. Homestay price increased 126% (200 →480\nCNY/night), and online cultural revenue accounted for 41% of total\nincome (Ciqikou Ancient Town case).\n2 Current Status of Technologies for Data\nAcquisition and Modeling\n2.1 Data Acquisition and Modeling\nTechnologies\n2.1.1 3D Laser Scanning. FARO Focus S350 realizes millimeter-\nlevel point cloud modeling. The thermal map of structural damage\nis generated with an average error margin <0.5% (Table 1) and meets\nthe requirements of heritage monitoring by UNESCO[4].\n2.1.2 Drone Oblique Photography. Drone Oblique Photography\nImages acquired from the air with 50-150m height cover multi-angle\nviews (Figure 4). The textured 3D model is generated by Context\nCapture software based on the stitching of images. The decay con-\ndition of buildings can be identified, for example, the area of wood\ndarkening suggests corrosion. Based on GIS software, the spatial\nFigure 4: Aerial Photography of Peng’s Traditional Dwelling\nby UA V. Photograph by Yue Yang.\ndistribution of buildings can be analyzed. The model is not only the\nrepresentation of the appearance of buildings, but also the reflection\nof the material condition through the color of the buildings, for ex-\nample, the area of wood darkening represents the risk of corrosion\n(Figure 5)[5]. Finally, based on the electronic map technology (GIS),\nthe scattered 3D models are aggregated into spatial distribution\nmaps. The building density, the age distribution, and other impor-\ntant data are intuitively displayed by the map, which provides a\nscientific basis for the planning of heritage conservation[6].\n2.1.3 Dynamic data   Peng’s Traditional Dwelling in Banan District,\nChongqing is presented below:\n3.1 Mechanism of Graded Protection\nAccording to the Technical Guidelines for Historical Building\nRestoration and Construction, we established an LOD Level-\nProtection Intensity Mapping Table (Table 4). For Peng’s Traditional\nDwelling, the buildings on the central axis used LOD400 modeling\nprecision (accuracy of 0.1mm, Figure 7), while the modeling of the\nsimplified peripheral buildings was used. The collaborative work\nmode of multi-angle UA V aerial photography and ground laser scan-\nning can produce high-precision 3D models with an error within\n554\x0cDigital Preservation of Chongqing Red Culture and Tourism Resource Development in Bayu Traditional Dwellings DSAI 2025, May 23–25, 2025, Wuhan, China\nTable 4: LOD Level-Protection Intensity Mapping.\nLOD\nLevel\nModeling Precision Protection Intensity Functional Replacement\nThreshold\nCase Areas\n400 0.1mm Freeze-style Protection ≤5% Main Hall, Principal Rooms, Living\nQuarters\n300 1mm Restricted Protection ≤30% Open Halls, Courtyards\n200 10mm Adaptive Reuse ≤60% Wing Rooms, Gatehouses\n100 50mm Regenerative Development ≤80% Auxiliary Rooms, Kitchens, Gardens,\nKitchens, Kitchens\n400 0.1mm Freeze-style Protection ≤5% Main Hall, Principal Rooms, Living\nQuarters\nFigure 7: Floor Plan of Peng’s Traditional Dwelling in Banan District, Chongqing. Photograph by Yue Yang.\nFigure 8: Restored Living Scenes via AR at Peng’s Dwelling. Photograph by Yue Yang.\n1cm. This method not only can reflect the details of the building\n(such as carvings, tiles) but also can discover hidden danger points\nof building structure (such as wood decay, hollow walls) through\nthermal imaging scanning, and the cost of inspection is reduced by\nmore than 50%.\n3.2 Living Inheritance Mode\nInherit historical archives combined with AR scene overlay technol-\nogy to reconstruct the main hall, principal rooms, living quarters,\nopen halls (Figure 8), extending dwell time to 30 minutes (visitors\nonly spend 15 minutes in  \n\n    Claim: When using advanced methods, cost of modeling per building is 3-5 times higher than traditional methods.\n\n    Task:\n    You are a strict data auditor. Evaluate the claim strictly against the provided context only. Do not use external knowledge.\n\n    Rate the claim using one of the following categories:\n    - Supported (output 5): The claim is directly stated or explicitly affirmed in the context.\n    - Strongly implied (output 4): The claim is not stated word‑for‑word, but can be logically and clearly inferred from multiple explicit statements in the context.\n    - Not mentioned (output 3): The context does not contain enough information to determine whether the claim is true or false.\n    - Contradicted (output 2): The context contains information that directly opposes the claim.\n    - Unclear (output 1): The evidence is ambiguous, contradictory within the context, or requires human judgment to resolve.\n\n    Instructions:\n    - Output only a single digit: 5,4,3,2,or 1.\n    - Do not include any explanation, punctuation, or extra text.\n\n    Your output:'}], 'model': 'deepseek-reasoner', 'stream': False}}
