ate of in-\ndigenous people is generally below 30%, and intangible cultural\nheritage skills are at risk of interruption in inheritance. It is neces-\nsary to achieve a closed loop of ”protection - benefit - participation”\nthrough policy guidance.\n4.2 Model bottlenecks and optimization\nrecommendations\n4.2.1 International cooperation experience. The ”government-led +\ncommunity crowdfunding” model used by Eastern European coun-\ntries such as Bulgaria and Hungary for historical buildings preser-\nvation can provide institutional design references for Chongqing.\nTsyganov (2022) found that Bulgaria’s digital authorization   and ethical problems.\nAcknowledgments\nGrant sponsor: Chongqing social science planning project, Grant\nno: 2024NDQN103.\nGrant sponsor: Competitive Research Programme, Grant no:\n202312608010.\nReferences\n[1] Huang, Y. (2015). Digital preservation and utilization of intangible cultural her-\nitage in the digital context. Cultural Heritage, (1), 34–42. DOI: 10.3969/j.issn.1674-\n0890.2015.01.001.\n[2] Ministry of Housing and Urban-Rural Development. (2021). Technical standard\nfor digitalization of historical buildings (JGJ/T 489-2021). China Architecture &\nBuilding Press. (Implemented from October 1, 2021)\n[3] Ministry of Culture and Tourism. (2023). Digital preservation of intangible cultural\nheritage: Digital resource collection and cataloguing (WH/T 99.1-2023–WH/T\n99.11-2023). Beijing: Author.\n[4] UNESCO. (2021). Operational guidelines for the implementation of the World\nHeritage Convention. UNESCO Press. Retrieved from https://whc.unesco.org/en/\nguidelines/\n[5] Li, X., Zhang, Q., & Wang, L. (2023). Digital twin for heritage preservation: A case\nstudy of Chongqing Anti-Japanese War sites. IEEE Transactions on Visualization\nand Computer Graphics, 29(5), 2134–2145. https://doi.org/10.1109/TVCG.2023.\n00001.\n[6] Costantino, C., Mantini, N., Benedetti, A. C., Bartolomei, C., & Predari, G.\n(2022).Digital and Territorial Trails Syst