l\ninformation), and archive storage breaks the limitation of phys-\nical media, realizing permanent traceability through blockchain\nauthentication in virtual space for lightweight commercial forms.\n5 Conclusion\nThis study proves that digital twins and metaverse technology can\naccommodate heritage preservation and tourism development. The\ntripartite framework of graded protection, hybrid reality interpreta-\ntion and community co-governance realizes accurate conservation\nand economy promotion. Future research can further explore light-\nweight modeling algorithms and decentralized Cultural tourism\nplatform to solve technical   integration. Digital Preservation of Intangible\nCultural Heritage: Digital Resource Collection and Cataloguing\n(WH/T 99.9-2023) Part 9: Traditional Craftsmanship, the craftsman-\nship process such as wooden construction and rammed earth tech-\nnology are recorded in sequence. Then, an interactive knowledge\ngraph is constructed to digitalize the recording of craftsmanship\nprocess.\n2.2 Digital Twin Platform Development\n2.2.1 Unity3D Engine Application. A digital twin system realizes\nthe real-time monitoring of visitors and AR virtual tour (Figure 6).\nThe historical cognition of visitors increased by 53% after the QR\ncode animation triggered by Unity3D engine.\n2.2.2 Metaverse Scenario Construction. Based on the NetEase Yao-\ntai architecture, a co-creation module of “Red Journey” is con-\nstructed in the virtual village. Users can upload wartime-themed\n552\x0cDigital Preservation of Chongqing Red Culture and Tourism Resource Development in Bayu Traditional Dwellings DSAI 2025, May 23–25, 2025, Wuhan, China\nTable 1: Core Data of Digital Preservation for Anti-Japanese War Vernacular Dwellings in Chongqing.\nBuilding Name Structure Type Termite\nIssue\nDigital Status Modeling Precision Data Error\nRate\nTechnical Methods\nHuangshan\nOfficial\nResidence\nBrick-Wood\nHybrid\nPresent Completed Millimeter-level\n(LOD400)\n0.32% FARO Laser Scanning +\nUA V Oblique Photography\nLiziba War Relics\nSite\nWooden\nStructure\nPresent Completed Millimeter-level\n(LOD350)\n0.45% Leica RTC360 + IoT\nHumidity Compensation\nZengjiayan\nNo.50\nBrick-Wood\nHybrid\nPresent Completed Submillimeter-level 0.28% Phase-Shift Scanning +\nHyperspectral Mapping\nBai Mansion Reinforced\nConcrete\nAbsent Completed Millimeter-level\n(LOD300)\n0.39% BIM Reverse Modeling +\nPoint Cloud Registration\nZhou Enlai’s\nFormer\nResidence\nWooden\nStructure\nPresent Completed Millimeter-level\n(LOD400)\n0.31% Multi-Station Point Cloud\nFusion + Structured Light\nNanshan War\nRelics\nMixed Structure Present Completed Centimeter-level\n(LOD150)\n0.78% Handheld Scanner +\nPanoramic Photography\nCiqikou Ancient\nTown\nWooden\nStructure\nPresent Completed Millimeter-level\n(LOD350)\n0.41% Mobile SLAM + Temporal\nModeling\nFotuguan War\nRelics\nStone-Wood\nHybrid\nAbsent Completed Millimeter-level\n(LOD300)\n0.37% UA V Swarm Scanning +\nEdge Computing\nLianglukou War\nRelics\nMixed Structure Present Completed Centimeter-level\n(LOD200)\n0.62% Lightweight Modeling +\nUGC Data Supplement\nFigure 5: 3D models of Peng’s Traditional Dwelling. Photo-\ngraph by Yue Yang.\nphotography and hand-painted drawings. The dynamic digital\narchive is generated by the uploaded files and connects the histori-\ncal narrative and   and ethical problems.\nAcknowledgments\nGrant sponsor: Chongqing social science planning project, Grant\nno: 2024NDQN103.\nGrant sponsor: Competitive Research Programme, Grant no:\n202312608010.\nReferences\n[1] Huang, Y. (2015). Digital preservation and utilization of intangible cultural her-\nitage in the digital context. Cultural Heritage, (1), 34–42. DOI: 10.3969/j.issn.1674-\n0890.2015.01.001.\n[2] Ministry of Housing and Urban-Rural Development. (2021). Technical standard\nfor digitalization of historical buildings (JGJ/T 489-2021). China Architecture &\nBuilding Press. (Implemented from October 1, 2021)\n[3] Ministry of Culture and Tourism. (2023). Digital preservation of intangible cultural\nheritage: Digital resource collection and cataloguing (WH/T 99.1-2023–WH/T\n99.11-2023). Beijing: Author.\n[4] UNESCO. (2021). Operational guidelines for the implementation of the World\nHeritage Convention. UNESCO Press. Retrieved from https://whc.unesco.org/en/\nguidelines/\n[5] Li, X., Zhang, Q., & Wang, L. (2023). Digital twin for heritage preservation: A case\nstudy of Chongqing Anti-Japanese War sites. IEEE Transactions on Visualization\nand Computer Graphics, 29(5), 2134–2145. https://doi.org/10.1109/TVCG.2023.\n00001.\n[6] Costantino, C., Mantini, N., Benedetti, A. C., Bartolomei, C., & Predari, G.\n(2022).Digital and Territorial Trails System for Developing Sustainable Tourism\nand Enhancing Cultural Heritage in Rural Areas: The Case of San Giovanni Lipioni,\nItaly.\n[7] Duan, X. (2020). Application of virtual reality in intangible cultural heritage\nprotection. Cultural and Art Research, (1), 19–23.\n[8] China Internet Society. (2023). 2023 China rural internet development report.\nPeople’s Posts and Telecommunications Press.\n[9] Tsyganov, M. (2022). Digital preservation of Balkan architectural heritage: Techni-\ncal innovations and community engagement. Sofia: Balkan Heritage Press. ISBN\n978-954-2976-22-1.\n557 \n\n    Claim: Intangible heritage skills (indigenous) are intended to be captured via living inheritance mode with AR, but not explicitly stated; implicit from section 3.2 and 4.1.3. However, no direct 'be_captured_by' for N08 to N15. I will omit to avoid over-imputation.\n\n    Task:\n    You are a strict data auditor. Evaluate the claim strictly against the provided context only. Do not use external knowledge.\n\n    Rate the claim using one of the following categories:\n    - Supported (output 5): The claim is directly stated or explicitly affirmed in the context.\n    - Strongly implied (output 4): The claim is not stated word‑for‑word, but can be logically and clearly 