 times higher than traditional methods (Table 3).\n2.4.2 Fragmented Scenarios in War Time. 85% of digital exhibitions\nonly passively show building exterior and wartime timeline but\nlack recreation of wartime life. For instance, when exhibiting bomb\nshelter, most cases only   rate of building damage\noccurred decreased by 17%.\n4 Online Tourism Resource Development\nPractices\n4.1 Comparative Case Analysis\nThrough the comparison of the effects of digitalization and tourism\ndevelopment of typical cases such as Huangshan Anti-Japanese War\nsite group, Liziba Park and Shanchengxiang (Table 5), the following\nthree common rules can be found.\n4.1.1 Technology-Driven Restoration Efficiency. Improvement\nHigh-def 3D scanning can improve restoration efficiency by 33%,\nbut the model precision needed to be balanced with real-time\nrendering performance (such as millions of polygons for a\nbuilding).\n4.1.2 Double-Edged Cultural Expression. Dynamic scene replica-\ntion can improve the understanding of history by more than 50%,\nbut excessive commercialization (such as punch point of Internet\ncelebrity) will reduce the number of EEG+ emotional signals by\n40%.\n556\x0cDigital Preservation of Chongqing Red Culture and Tourism Resource Development in Bayu Traditional Dwellings DSAI 2025, May 23–25, 2025, Wuhan, China\nTable 6: Intergenerational Comparison of Conservation Technologies.\nTechnological\nGeneration\nRepresentative Methods Data Dimensions Error Rate Storage\nDuration\nInformation\nInteractivity\n1st (1930s) Paper-based Surveying 2D Planar 5-8% 50-80 years Unidirectional\nRecording\n2nd (1980s) Total Station + Film\nPhotography\n2.5D Facades (Elevation +\nPartial Depth Data)\n2-3% 30-50 years Limited Retrieval\n3rd (2020s) Laser Scanning + Digital Twin 5D Spacetime (3D + Time\n+ Environment)\n0.5% Permanent\nCloud Storage\nReal-time\nInteraction\n4.1.3 Living Heritage Transmission Crisis. The return rate of in-\ndigenous people is generally below 30%, and intangible cultural\nheritage skills are at risk of interruption in inheritance. It is neces-\nsary to achieve a closed loop of ”protection - benefit - participation”\nthrough policy guidance.\n4.2 Model bottlenecks and optimization\nrecommendations\n4.2.1 International cooperation experience. The ”government-led +\ncommunity crowdfunding” model used by Eastern European coun-\ntries such as Bulgaria and Hungary for historical buildings preser-\nvation can provide institutional design references for Chongqing.\nTsyganov (2022) found that Bulgaria’s digital authorization   sys-\ntem for intangible cultural heritage (ICH) skills raised traditional\nartisans’ income by 40%(Reitz et al., 2021), which can be refer-\nenced for the ”resident-merchant symbiosis” model in Chongqing’s\nShanchengxiang[9].\n4.2.2 Contradictions in Functional Adjustment. Commercialization\nof historical buildings meets a ”preservation-utilization” contradic-\ntion. It is proposed that strict prohibition on functional change is\nimplemented in core protected zones, while lightweight commer-\ncial form are allowed in peripheral areas (e.g., bookstores, cultural\nshops).\n4.2.3 Technical Silos and Data Standardization. More than 70%\nprojects are trapped in fragmented data format and system interop-\nerability issues. It is urgent to establish cross-platform standards\n(e.g., unified 3D model format, GLTF or OpenUSD), so that the his-\ntorical buildings can achieve ”one-time acquisition, multi-scenario\napplication”.\n4.2.4 Policy Implementation Gaps. Although provincial level has\nissued cultural heritage protection regulations, district level en-\ncounters problem of fund misallocation and regulation gaps. It is\ncritical to establish a transparent management system, which is\ncomposed of digital supervision platforms (e.g., Suzhou’s integrated\ndigital supervision mode) and third party evaluations.\n4.3 Analysis of intergenerational evolution of\nconservation technology\nChongqing’s historical building conservation technology from 1930\nto 2023 is compared (Table 6), which shows the advantages of digital\ntechnology in terms of information preservation evolution from\n2D planar to 5D spacetime (3D space + timeline + environmental\ninformation), and archive storage breaks the limitation of phys-\nical media, realizing permanent traceability through blockchain\nauthentication in virtual space for lightweight commercial forms.\n5 Conclusion\nThis study proves that digital twins and metaverse technology can\naccommodate heritage preservation and tourism development. The\ntripartite framework of graded protection, hybrid reality interpreta-\ntion and community co-governance realizes accurate conservation\nand economy promotion. Future research can further explore light-\nweight modeling algorithms and decentralized Cultural tourism\nplatform to solve technical  \n\nStatement: Historical knowledge retention metric (58% improvement) is transferred by Traditional exhibition methods\n\nTask:\nYou are a data auditor and need to determine if this statement is correct. Evaluate this statement strictly based on the provided background information. Do not use external knowledge.\n\nInstructions:\n- Output only a single digit: 5 or 1, 5 for True, 1 for False.\n- Do not include any explanation, punctuation, or extra text.\n\nYour output:'}], 'model': 'deepseek-reasoner', 'stream': False}}
