pping\nBai Mansion Reinforced\nConcrete\nAbsent Completed Millimeter-level\n(LOD300)\n0.39% BIM Reverse Modeling +\nPoint Cloud Registration\nZhou Enlai’s\nFormer\nResidence\nWooden\nStructure\nPresent Completed Millimeter-level\n(LOD400)\n0.31% Multi-Station Point Cloud\nFusion + Structured Light\nNanshan War\nRelics\nMixed Structure Present Completed Centimeter-level\n(LOD150)\n0.78% Handheld Scanner +\nPanoramic Photography\nCiqikou Ancient\nTown\nWooden\nStructure\nPresent Completed Millimeter-level\n(LOD350)\n0.41% Mobile SLAM + Temporal\nModeling\nFotuguan War\nRelics\nStone-Wood\nHybrid\nAbsent Completed Millimeter-level\n(LOD300)\n0.37% UA V Swarm Scanning +\nEdge Computing\nLianglukou War\nRelics\nMixed Structure Present Completed Centimeter-level\n(LOD200)\n0.62% Lightweight Modeling +\nUGC Data Supplement\nFigure 5: 3D models of Peng’s Traditional Dwelling. Photo-\ngraph by Yue Yang.\nphotography and hand-painted drawings. The dynamic digital\narchive is generated by the uploaded files and connects the histori-\ncal narrative and   sys-\ntem for intangible cultural heritage (ICH) skills raised traditional\nartisans’ income by 40%(Reitz et al., 2021), which can be refer-\nenced for the ”resident-merchant symbiosis” model in Chongqing’s\nShanchengxiang[9].\n4.2.2 Contradictions in Functional Adjustment. Commercialization\nof historical buildings meets a ”preservation-utilization” contradic-\ntion. It is proposed that strict prohibition on functional change is\nimplemented in core protected zones, while lightweight commer-\ncial form are allowed in peripheral areas (e.g., bookstores, cultural\nshops).\n4.2.3 Technical Silos and Data Standardization. More than 70%\nprojects are trapped in fragmented data format and system interop-\nerability issues. It is urgent to establish cross-platform standards\n(e.g., unified 3D model format, GLTF or OpenUSD), so that the his-\ntorical buildings can achieve ”one-time acquisition, multi-scenario\napplication”.\n4.2.4 Policy Implementation Gaps. Although provincial level has\nissued cultural heritage protection regulations, district level en-\ncounters problem of fund misallocation and regulation gaps. It is\ncritical to establish a transparent management system, which is\ncomposed of digital supervision platforms (e.g., Suzhou’s integrated\ndigital supervision mode) and third party evaluations.\n4.3 Analysis of intergenerational evolution of\nconservation technology\nChongqing’s historical building conservation technology from 1930\nto 2023 is compared (Table 6), which shows the advantages of digital\ntechnology in terms of information preservation evolution from\n2D planar to 5D spacetime (3D space + timeline + environmental\ninformation), and archive storage breaks the limitation of phys-\nical media, realizing permanent traceability through blockchain\nauthentication in virtual space for lightweight commercial forms.\n5 Conclusion\nThis study proves that digital twins and metaverse technology can\naccommodate heritage preservation and tourism development. The\ntripartite framework of graded protection, hybrid reality interpreta-\ntion and community co-governance realizes accurate conservation\nand economy promotion. Future research can further explore light-\nweight modeling algorithms and decentralized Cultural tourism\nplatform to solve technical   interactive experience.\n2.3 User Experience Evaluation System\n2.3.1 Multimodal Interaction Testing. Tobii Pro eye tracker and\nEmotiv EEG device, the distribution of visitor’s attention and the\nemotion of visitors in VR scenario are quantified. The data show\nthat the historical cognition of visitors increased by 53% after the\nimplementation of dynamic narrative system, and the excessive\ncommercial operation would decrease the number of EEG positive\nsignals by 40%[7](Table 2).\n2.3.2 A/B Testing Optimization. Compared with traditional exhibi-\ntion, VR exhibition hall extended average stay time for 22 minutes\nand elevated repeat visitation rate by 18% (Table 2).\n2.4 Challenges in Digital Application\n2.4.1 Disparity between Urban and Rural Areas in Application of\nTechnology. There are notable disparities in digital preservation im-\nplementation between urban and rural areas of Chongqing. LOD400\nmodeling precision is adopted for projects in the central urban dis-\ntricts where all architectural ornaments such as window and door\ncarvings can be depicted. In comparison, modeling precision of\ncounty-level projects is restricted to LOD200 models that only re-\ntain basic outlines of buildings, and data interoperability rate is\nbelow 30%. As analyzed in 2023 China Rural Internet Development\nReport [ 8], r technical challenges that rural areas are facing are\nsummarized as follows: (1) point cloud data transmission failure\nrate in rural areas is as high as 18.7% (vs 2.3% in the urban cores)\n(2) although digital technologies can reduce surveying errors by\n92%, when using advanced methods, cost of modeling per building\nis 3-5 times higher than traditional methods (Table 3).\n2.4.2 Fragmented Scenarios in War Time. 85% of digital exhibitions\nonly passively show building exterior and wartime timeline but\nlack recreation of wartime life. For instance, when exhibiting bomb\nshelter, most cases only  \n\nStatement: Wooden construction craftsmanship skills is captured by Interactive knowledge graph\n\nTask:\nYou are a data auditor and need to determine if this statement is correct. Evaluate this statement strictly based on the provided background information. Do not use external knowledge.\n\nInstructions:\n- Output only a single digit: 5 or 1, 5 for True, 1 for False.\n- Do not include any explanation, punctuation, or extra text.\n\nYour output:'}], 'model': 'deepseek-reasoner', 'stream': False}}
