2026-05-28 03:05:35,466 - openai._base_client - DEBUG - Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/chat/completions', 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-c2d1adb5-3569-4972-8c4d-bdd2cb4d02b4', 'security': {'bearer_auth': True}, 'content': None, 'json_data': {'messages': [{'role': 'user', 'content': "\nContext:  sys-\ntem for intangible cultural heritage (ICH) skills raised traditional\nartisans’ income by 40%(Reitz et al., 2021), which can be refer-\nenced for the ”resident-merchant symbiosis” model in Chongqing’s\nShanchengxiang[9].\n4.2.2 Contradictions in Functional Adjustment. Commercialization\nof historical buildings meets a ”preservation-utilization” contradic-\ntion. It is proposed that strict prohibition on functional change is\nimplemented in core protected zones, while lightweight commer-\ncial form are allowed in peripheral areas (e.g., bookstores, cultural\nshops).\n4.2.3 Technical Silos and Data Standardization. More than 70%\nprojects are trapped in fragmented data format and system interop-\nerability issues. It is urgent to establish cross-platform standards\n(e.g., unified 3D model format, GLTF or OpenUSD), so that the his-\ntorical buildings can achieve ”one-time acquisition, multi-scenario\napplication”.\n4.2.4 Policy Implementation Gaps. Although provincial level has\nissued cultural heritage protection regulations, district level en-\ncounters problem of fund misallocation and regulation gaps. It is\ncritical to establish a transparent management system, which is\ncomposed of digital supervision platforms (e.g., Suzhou’s integrated\ndigital supervision mode) and third party evaluations.\n4.3 Analysis of intergenerational evolution of\nconservation technology\nChongqing’s historical building conservation technology from 1930\nto 2023 is compared (Table 6), which shows the advantages of digital\ntechnology in terms of information preservation evolution from\n2D planar to 5D spacetime (3D space + timeline + environmental\ninformation), and archive storage breaks the limitation of phys-\nical media, realizing permanent traceability through blockchain\nauthentication in virtual space for lightweight commercial forms.\n5 Conclusion\nThis study proves that digital twins and metaverse technology can\naccommodate heritage preservation and tourism development. The\ntripartite framework of graded protection, hybrid reality interpreta-\ntion and community co-governance realizes accurate conservation\nand economy promotion. Future research can further explore light-\nweight modeling algorithms and decentralized Cultural tourism\nplatform to solve technical   and ethical problems.\nAcknowledgments\nGrant sponsor: Chongqing social science planning project, Grant\nno: 2024NDQN103.\nGrant sponsor: Competitive Research Programme, Grant no:\n202312608010.\nReferences\n[1] Huang, Y. (2015). Digital preservation and utilization of intangible cultural her-\nitage in the digital context. Cultural Heritage, (1), 34–42. DOI: 10.3969/j.issn.1674-\n0890.2015.01.001.\n[2] Ministry of Housing and Urban-Rural Development. (2021). Technical standard\nfor digitalization of historical buildings (JGJ/T 489-2021). China Architecture &\nBuilding Press. (Implemented from October 1, 2021)\n[3] Ministry of Culture and Tourism. (2023). Digital preservation of intangible cultural\nheritage: Digital resource collection and cataloguing (WH/T 99.1-2023–WH/T\n99.11-2023). Beijing: Author.\n[4] UNESCO. (2021). Operational guidelines for the implementation of the World\nHeritage Convention. UNESCO Press. Retrieved from https://whc.unesco.org/en/\nguidelines/\n[5] Li, X., Zhang, Q., & Wang, L. (2023). Digital twin for heritage preservation: A case\nstudy of Chongqing Anti-Japanese War sites. IEEE Transactions on Visualization\nand Computer Graphics, 29(5), 2134–2145. https://doi.org/10.1109/TVCG.2023.\n00001.\n[6] Costantino, C., Mantini, N., Benedetti, A. C., Bartolomei, C., & Predari, G.\n(2022).Digital and Territorial Trails System for Developing Sustainable Tourism\nand Enhancing Cultural Heritage in Rural Areas: The Case of San Giovanni Lipioni,\nItaly.\n[7] Duan, X. (2020). Application of virtual reality in intangible cultural heritage\nprotection. Cultural and Art Research, (1), 19–23.\n[8] China Internet Society. (2023). 2023 China rural internet development report.\nPeople’s Posts and Telecommunications Press.\n[9] Tsyganov, M. (2022). Digital preservation of Balkan architectural heritage: Techni-\ncal innovations and community engagement. Sofia: Balkan Heritage Press. ISBN\n978-954-2976-22-1.\n557  Digital Preservation of Chongqing Red Culture and Tourism\nResource Development in Bayu Traditional Dwellings\nYue Yang∗\nChongqing Institute of Engineering\nChongqing, China\n376521977@qq.com\nBin Chen\nChongqing Institute of Engineering\nChongqing, China\n149209173@qq.com\nAbstract\nAs the wartime capital of the Anti-Japanese War, Bayu area owns\ntraditional dwellings with wartime culture and architectural wis-\ndom left in. Focusing on the Chongqing Red Culture heritage, this\npaper explores digital representation approaches and tourism de-\nvelopment paths of Bayu traditional dwellings through field survey\nand digital technology analysis. Based on 3D laser scanning, VR\n(Virtual Reality) and big data, we collect architectural data and\nconduct comparative analysis on Typical Cases such as Huangshan\nAnti-Japanese War Site Cluster and Liziba War Relics Park. The\nmain results are as follows: (1) Digital technology can improve\narchitectural data collection accuracy (error rate <0.5%) and visi-\ntors’ immersion in virtual experience (the VR stay rate has been\nincreased by 40%); (2) Existing development models have the fol-\nlowing problems: too much functional replacement and lack of\ntechnical specifications; (3) A new framework of “graded protection-\nliving inheritance-digital twin” is proposed. Under the support of\nAR (Augmented Reality) guided tour and metaverse scenario, the\nframework develops dynamic narrative system (e.g. reconstruc-\ntion of bombing scene) under the standard of Digital Protection\nof Intangible Cultural Heritage (2023) [ 3] and improves historical\nknowledge retention rate by 58%. Case studies show that, compared\nwith traditional methods, this framework can increase tourism rev-\nenue by 18%-25% and reduce restoration cost by 30%. This research\nprovides theoretical basis and reference for the sustainable use of\nChongqing Red Culture heritage.\nCCS Concepts\n• Applied computing →Arts and humanities; Architecture (build-\nings).\nKeywords\nTraditional dwellings, Chongqing Red Culture, Digital Preservation,\nCultural-Tourism Integration, digital twin technology\nACM Reference Format:\nYue Yang and Bin Chen. 2025.  \n\nStatement: Indigenous people's intangible cultural heritage skills is difficult to capture due to Risk of interruption in inheritance of intangible skills\n\nTask:\nYou are a data auditor and need to determine if this statement is correct. Evaluate this statement strictly based on the provided background information. Do not use external knowledge.\n\nInstructions:\n- Output only a single digit: 5 or 1, 5 for True, 1 for False.\n- Do not include any explanation, punctuation, or extra text.\n\nYour output:"}], 'model': 'deepseek-reasoner', 'stream': False}}
