s, and data interoperability rate is\nbelow 30%. As analyzed in 2023 China Rural Internet Development\nReport [ 8], r technical challenges that rural areas are facing are\nsummarized as follows: (1) point cloud data transmission failure\nrate in rural areas is as high as 18.7% (vs 2.3% in the urban cores)\n(2) although digital technologies can reduce surveying errors by\n92%, when using advanced methods, cost of modeling per building\nis 3-5 times higher than traditional methods (Table 3).\n2.4.2 Fragmented Scenarios in War Time. 85% of digital exhibitions\nonly passively show building exterior and wartime timeline but\nlack recreation of wartime life. For instance, when exhibiting bomb\nshelter, most cases only   Digital Preservation of Chongqing Red Culture and Tourism\nResource Development in Bayu Traditional Dwellings\nYue Yang∗\nChongqing Institute of Engineering\nChongqing, China\n376521977@qq.com\nBin Chen\nChongqing Institute of Engineering\nChongqing, China\n149209173@qq.com\nAbstract\nAs the wartime capital of the Anti-Japanese War, Bayu area owns\ntraditional dwellings with wartime culture and architectural wis-\ndom left in. Focusing on the Chongqing Red Culture heritage, this\npaper explores digital representation approaches and tourism de-\nvelopment paths of Bayu traditional dwellings through field survey\nand digital technology analysis. Based on 3D laser scanning, VR\n(Virtual Reality) and big data, we collect architectural data and\nconduct comparative analysis on Typical Cases such as Huangshan\nAnti-Japanese War Site Cluster and Liziba War Relics Park. The\nmain results are as follows: (1) Digital technology can improve\narchitectural data collection accuracy (error rate <0.5%) and visi-\ntors’ immersion in virtual experience (the VR stay rate has been\nincreased by 40%); (2) Existing development models have the fol-\nlowing problems: too much functional replacement and lack of\ntechnical specifications; (3) A new framework of “graded protection-