this could be that VR\nallows for spatial interaction and the use of interactive instructional methods (Babu et al.,\n2018). In our case, participants from the pilot study acknowledged the user experience of the\nimmersive VR training system, where realism, ease of use and ease of learning received high\nscores (seeFigure 6).\nThird, the comparative study tested the immersive VR training system against a\ntraditional training method (a safety manual). Results show   performance. This is different\nfrom the literature, where in most cases, VR safety training is assessed by the effect on\nknowledge acquisition. Last, this study provides extra evidence about comparing VR and\ntraditional training to the literature. This study shows that VR and traditional training may\nperform similarly in immediate knowledge acquisition. However, VR training outperforms\ntraditional training in the long term because more safety knowledge can be retained following\nVR training.\nThese findings have several implications. First, this study demonstrates the effectiveness\nof VR-based excavation safety training in terms of immediate knowledge acquisition,\npractical performance and knowledge retention. In addition, VR outperforms traditional\napproaches in the case of knowledge retention. The construction industry can take from this\nto test and apply VR solutions into actual practice for excavation safety. By applying a more\neffective training solution, the construction industry  \n\nStatement: Real-time hazard identification performance is difficult to capture due to Poor knowledge retention in traditional training\n\nTask:\nYou are a data auditor and need to determine if this statement is correct. Evaluate this statement strictly based on the provided background information. Do not use external knowledge.\n\nInstructions:\n- Output only a single digit: 5 or 1, 5 for True, 1 for False.\n- Do not include any explanation, punctuation, or extra text.\n\nYour output:'}], 'model': 'deepseek-